WEBVTT

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:08.750
What would you rather, four?

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:17.620
I got here. I thought you were gone.

00:03:17.700 --> 00:03:19.340
I was in the shower. What are you doing here?

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.120
You have dry rot.

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:21.940
Don't get personal, O'Connell.

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:23.700
Mmm, you made coffee.

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.240
Yeah, so?

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:25.740
Great.

00:03:28.170 --> 00:03:29.960
Hey, what exactly is dry rot?

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.260
The architectural equivalent of a trench mouth?

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:33.340
More or less.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:37.800
Wonderful. I'm living in a cabin infested with a horrible timber disease.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.420
Well, I just had to replace the planking, reseal and re-shingle a few places.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:43.300
Well, how long is that going to take?

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:44.140
A few days.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:47.090
What am I supposed to do until then? Suffer in silence?

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.110
When do you do anything in silence, Flashman?

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:53.090
Hey, let's be clear about something, O'Connell. There's nothing wrong with my roof.

00:03:53.210 --> 00:03:55.910
I don't have a roof. Whatever's rotting up there is rotting on your roof.

00:03:56.270 --> 00:03:56.870
What's your point?

00:03:57.380 --> 00:03:59.980
The point is your roof has caused my heating bill to go through the stratus...

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.890
And for the foreseeable future, I'm going to be sleeping in an arctic breezeway.

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:05.280
Well, I'll bring you a blanket.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:06.400
No, you're missing the point.

00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:09.480
As a landlord, you have a responsibility for the general welfare of your tenant,

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.140
an obligation I think any reasonable man or woman would feel has been sorely violated.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.060
I want a rebate.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.440
Hmm, your dreams, Fleischman.

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:22.120
Besides, the great state of Alaska pays for your rent.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:23.360
You don't even pay a dime.

00:04:23.700 --> 00:04:25.600
Hey, that's exactly what it's worth.

00:04:26.040 --> 00:04:29.970
I'll tell you what, you fix whatever's wrong with my truck, and I'm...

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.240
I'm willing to overlook any gross negligence on your part.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.240
There's nothing wrong with your truck except that you flood the engine.

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:36.760
You should just lighten up on the gas.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.110
Don't flood the engine, O'Connell. I happen to have a very light foot.

00:04:41.270 --> 00:04:42.330
Fine, I'll give you a lift.

00:04:43.500 --> 00:04:43.980
I'll walk.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:46.640
Ooh, man of character.

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:04.070
Dreams are postcards from the subconscious, inner self to outer self.

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.300
Right brain trying to cross that moat to the left.

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:09.780
All too often, they come back unread.

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.360
Return to sender, address unknown.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:16.500
That's a shame, too, because there's a whole different world out there.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:19.020
Or in here, depending on your point of view.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:24.870
Indeed, for all we know, this very moment could be nothing more than vapors of our own imagination.

00:05:25.070 --> 00:05:29.860
As Bertrand Russell mused, I do not believe that I am now dreaming.

00:05:30.350 --> 00:05:32.050
But I cannot prove that I am not.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.180
Point being, there's more to these nocturnal journeys

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.270
than has previously been considered or accepted.

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.900
Man, I hardly recognize you. What happened to the leathers?

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.360
Oh, I don't need them. I sold the Harley.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:51.800
Yeah?

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:55.420
Oh, yeah, last year, the leather, the Harley, it was all part of my...

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:56.200
Quest.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:05:59.980
You really look...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:00.400
You look good.

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:01.080
Bourgeois.

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:01.900
Thanks.

00:06:02.380 --> 00:06:02.820
Hungry?

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:04.250
Let's eat.

00:06:06.770 --> 00:06:09.230
So Mom says it's been three years since Dad died.

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:10.790
Why shouldn't I re-marry?

00:06:11.300 --> 00:06:12.360
I don't have any problem with that.

00:06:12.940 --> 00:06:15.960
Anyway, she says she wants to make a new start, wipe the slate clean.

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:18.240
And then she has confession.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:20.220
Which brings me to this.

00:06:22.990 --> 00:06:24.370
36,000 bucks?

00:06:25.240 --> 00:06:28.000
When Dad died, he left $60,000 to his sons.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:29.980
But unbeknownst to me, Mom altered me.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.520
with the will. She left the ass off. Yeah, smart. Anyway, it seems like mom found out about dad's

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.540
other family, your family, didn't sit too well with her. Don't take it personally. She was just

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:47.040
lashing out. No, no, I understand. Anyway, of the $60,000, 30 of it's yours, and the $6,000 is

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:54.030
interest. I can't take that, Menard. Why not? Because your dad left you $60,000. My dad left me

00:06:54.030 --> 00:06:59.440
nothing. He's one and the same guy. No, no, no. My dad was a bum. He was totally irresponsible.

00:07:00.120 --> 00:07:01.960
every buck he ever had. He wouldn't have liked leaving

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.040
any money behind, that's for sure, especially

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:05.820
to me. Now, your dad

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:08.410
physically was the same guy, but spiritually

00:07:08.410 --> 00:07:10.350
totally different animal. Solid,

00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:11.790
dependable. Properly mobile.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:13.920
Mortgage, credit cards, life insurance.

00:07:14.120 --> 00:07:15.040
Term and hold life.

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:19.820
That belongs to you. Chris, I got everything from a father

00:07:19.820 --> 00:07:21.620
a son can buy. Security,

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:23.340
love, quality time.

00:07:24.850 --> 00:07:28.400
You got nothing, at the very least. You deserve this.

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:37.940
Marilyn, did you tell Encyclopedia Britannica that they can pay the postage this time?

00:07:38.140 --> 00:07:41.420
I didn't order a yearbook. I don't want a yearbook. I'm not going to pay for a yearbook.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:42.980
Thank you.

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:48.410
Hey, Marilyn.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.760
A little jumpy, aren't we?

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.420
Mr. Adams here.

00:07:54.740 --> 00:07:55.300
Thank you, Marilyn.

00:07:55.960 --> 00:07:57.800
Have we ever considered Xanax?

00:07:57.920 --> 00:07:58.700
Look, what are you doing here?

00:07:59.200 --> 00:07:59.980
You know, I love...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:05.380
you've done with the place really although you know the faux distressed look is a little bit

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:09.510
of a cliche yeah well you really hit me where i live adam now would you please leave oh nice

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:14.800
this is how you treat a friend you kidnapped me your your wife held me in chains all right

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:21.480
acquaintance where is eve anyways her billy reuben levels all screwed up she had to go to geneva

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.200
for a lamb's blood treatment oh really what a pity send her my best will you please get out of my

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:29.980
office before I have to disinfect. God knows what exotic microbes you're dragged in.

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:31.600
How much money do you have?

00:08:32.140 --> 00:08:32.440
What?

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:33.800
I need $100.

00:08:35.250 --> 00:08:36.050
Excuse me?

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:38.760
What are you, deaf? Give me $100.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:40.420
I'm not giving you $100!

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:43.380
I suppose you have better uses for the money.

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:46.430
Orchestra seats to Miss Saigon, maybe.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:48.210
Dinner at Le Cirque after.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:49.780
What do you need it for, anyway?

00:08:50.130 --> 00:08:54.250
I don't have to! My insurance premium's due.

00:08:54.470 --> 00:08:55.990
Oh, your insurance premium.

00:08:56.520 --> 00:08:59.580
Now, come on. Just give me whatever you got in your wallet.

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:01.100
I'm not giving you anything, understand?

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:03.640
In case you're missing the underlying sentiment, you're not welcome.

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:05.770
Go. Goodbye. Have a nice day.

00:09:06.320 --> 00:09:07.020
What, Marilyn?

00:09:07.690 --> 00:09:08.190
Elaine's here.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:09.220
Elaine who?

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:10.200
You're Elaine.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:14.760
Hi.

00:09:17.920 --> 00:09:18.300
Elaine.

00:09:19.180 --> 00:09:20.000
How are you, Joel?

00:09:21.210 --> 00:09:22.710
I'm fine. What are you doing here?

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:25.100
Well, I guess I came to see you.

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.300
Oh. Uh, where's Dwayne?

00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:39.330
your husband the judge dwight oh dwight where's he he died geez well it wasn't a total surprise

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:48.140
he had a history of coronary artery disease hey uh i i don't know what to say why don't you start

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:56.030
by offering the lady a chair where were you raised fleischman a barn out what about my money out now

00:10:01.600 --> 00:10:03.300
I'm sorry, he just walked into my office.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:04.520
I mean, I hardly know him.

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:06.150
That's okay.

00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.090
You know, Elaine, this is a...

00:10:09.980 --> 00:10:10.620
A surprise.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:11.740
Yeah.

00:10:12.260 --> 00:10:12.980
I didn't call.

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:14.860
No, no, you didn't.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.880
I was afraid you'd tell me not to come.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:17.720
Well, I would have.

00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:19.240
Yes, I would have said don't come.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.760
And I knew if I thought about it, I wouldn't have the courage to do it.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:24.660
So I jumped in the cab, went to Kennedy, bought a ticket.

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:26.120
Next thing I knew, I was in Anchorage.

00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:26.620
Now here.

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:28.490
I don't even have a suitcase.

00:10:30.160 --> 00:10:32.140
Elaine. Look, Joel, please don't say anything.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:34.940
I mean, I know how you must feel. I do.

00:10:35.480 --> 00:10:36.800
The way I broke it off,

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.880
the dear Joel letter. Don't get

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:43.830
frostbite. I'm so

00:10:44.760 --> 00:10:47.170
sorry, Joel. Look at you write

00:10:47.170 --> 00:10:49.750
that. After all the years we were

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.210
together, we had a relationship.

00:10:51.910 --> 00:10:53.950
We had a history, and you end it with don't get

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:56.410
frostbite? Yeah, you're still angry.

00:10:57.910 --> 00:10:58.760
Bitter, resentful,

00:10:58.820 --> 00:10:59.420
humiliated.

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:03.720
Why'd you come? What are you looking for?

00:11:03.860 --> 00:11:04.720
What did you think would happen?

00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:11.160
I don't know. I just wanted to see.

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:14.980
You just wanted to see me?

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:16.890
Maybe it was a mistake.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:19.920
There's no maybe about it. It was a mistake.

00:11:20.120 --> 00:11:20.860
It was a big mistake.

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:46.340
I mean, what does she expect? Passion?

00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:50.130
She dumps me for a retiree who goes belly up,

00:11:50.170 --> 00:11:52.530
and then she wants some kind of emotional refund.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.540
What, I'm supposed to welcome her with open arms?

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:57.380
What is this?

00:11:58.370 --> 00:11:59.970
I mean, I'm about as forgiving as...

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.240
I mean, by New York standards, I'm more than nice.

00:12:02.550 --> 00:12:04.090
I send my mother flowers for her birthday.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.590
I recycle. I make regular contributions to worthy causes.

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.990
Is this supposed to be a mushroom?

00:12:10.630 --> 00:12:12.990
Certain things, however, cannot be forgiven.

00:12:14.240 --> 00:12:16.280
The total dismemberment of my emotional well-being,

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:18.580
the annihilation of any kind of future happiness,

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:20.940
they safely go into the not-to-be-forgiven category.

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:23.390
That was everything, fellas.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:28.570
This isn't a meal. This is 20 questions. Pray tell.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.070
Shoelace, roadkill, color swatch?

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:36.200
Surely this is not a tomato.

00:12:36.610 --> 00:12:37.630
If there's a problem.

00:12:37.870 --> 00:12:39.530
Oh, no, no, no, there's no problem.

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.930
As long as you don't expect people to eat this.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:44.280
Come on, Fleischman, get your coat.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:45.020
Huh?

00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:46.970
I'm not paying for this swill.

00:12:47.450 --> 00:12:49.410
You don't have any money, I'm paying for it.

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:50.830
You think you could do better, Fran?

00:12:51.170 --> 00:12:52.310
He could do better.

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:54.690
It's easy to take pot shots from the sidelines.

00:12:54.750 --> 00:12:55.930
You'll be singing a different tune

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.030
in the heat of the lunch hour rush.

00:12:58.250 --> 00:12:59.960
Oh, I'm trying to...

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:06.590
trembling let me tell you something in 48 hours i would have people lined up outside this joint

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:13.350
begging to get in you wouldn't want to put a little wager on that would you a hundred dollars

00:13:13.350 --> 00:13:21.170
i'll show you to the kitchen oh no no no no that won't be necessary i'll just follow the stench

00:13:21.170 --> 00:13:29.980
friend of yours nice job flashman a woman comes to you and griefing you

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:31.130
Slam the door in her face?

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:32.490
Please, O'Connell.

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:34.950
She's in my truck right now, sobbing her eyes out.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:35.720
Wait a minute.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:38.070
I think you're confusing the injured parties here.

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:39.990
I'm not the one who broke off a 20-year relationship

00:13:39.990 --> 00:13:41.610
through the auspices of the U.S. mail.

00:13:42.060 --> 00:13:43.160
I'm the one who got kicked in the teeth,

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:44.840
stabbed in the back, left in the lurch.

00:13:44.840 --> 00:13:46.140
Pick your metaphor, I'm it.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:47.540
Grow up, Fleischman.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:48.560
Grow up?

00:13:48.820 --> 00:13:50.140
Yes, for once in your life.

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:51.040
Can't you think of something else

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:52.280
besides your own selfish concerns?

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.580
No, frankly, I can't.

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:55.580
This may come as a surprise to you, O'Connell,

00:13:55.620 --> 00:13:56.520
but men have feelings, too.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:59.080
I might have been mutilated by that font of human kindness

00:13:59.080 --> 00:13:59.900
currently overflowing.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:06.500
your truck look about selfish she did exactly what she wanted she bet on a

00:14:06.500 --> 00:14:11.560
geriatric horse that fell down in the first turn well I can sympathize with

00:14:11.560 --> 00:14:14.840
such unfortunate I'll be a predictable course of events but that's just about

00:14:14.840 --> 00:14:19.060
as far as I plan to extend myself let me ask you a question what did she say

00:14:19.060 --> 00:14:25.270
she wanted it's pretty obvious don't you think enlighten me she wants me back

00:14:25.270 --> 00:14:29.610
you would think a thing like that what is that supposed to mean did she say she

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:29.870
wanted

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:35.050
you back what did she say she said she wanted to see me right because you're her friend you're

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:40.550
her dearest friend her closest friend in the whole world and she suffered an appalling tragedy and

00:14:40.990 --> 00:14:46.610
in her despair in her darkest hour of life she comes to you knowing how much resistance she's

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:50.030
going to meet knowing that in all likelihood she's going to have to bear the brunt of your

00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:54.950
suppressed rage still she comes to see you joel fleischman that's how important you are to her

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:59.610
she's reaching out fleischman for a familiar shoulder for compassion

00:15:02.940 --> 00:15:04.890
Just let her know she still has a friend.

00:15:05.360 --> 00:15:07.520
Be a mensch.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:11.150
I believe the word you're looking for is mensch.

00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:14.220
Whatever. Be one.

00:15:17.530 --> 00:15:17.930
Mustard?

00:15:19.050 --> 00:15:19.450
Frenches.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:22.220
The only mustard for dogs.

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:25.140
Do you have any sauerkraut?

00:15:28.290 --> 00:15:29.770
Bernard, have you ever been to Africa?

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:31.560
Nope. You?

00:15:32.290 --> 00:15:32.630
No.

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:34.020
Why do you ask?

00:15:34.830 --> 00:15:37.390
Well, I've been having these really strange dreams lately.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:42.960
Africa, dancers, drums, just so real, so vivid.

00:15:44.130 --> 00:15:44.390
Yeah.

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:45.980
You been having them too?

00:15:46.640 --> 00:15:46.840
Nope.

00:15:47.750 --> 00:15:49.160
I don't dream much anymore.

00:15:50.440 --> 00:15:51.160
No dreams?

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:55.000
Well, sometimes I dream, but they're so mundane, so pallid,

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.680
like mailing a letter, mowing the lawn,

00:15:58.270 --> 00:15:59.910
picking up my suit from the dry cleaners.

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:02.970
And I used to have really vivid dreams.

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:04.920
I mean, dreams Van Gogh would envy.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:07.610
Lush, florid, stirring dreams.

00:16:08.130 --> 00:16:08.670
Now even more.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:11.260
Why?

00:16:12.010 --> 00:16:13.150
I don't know. Something's missing.

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:17.630
It's like my brain has been stripped clean, you know, denuded.

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:21.180
The rainforest in my psyche has been sprayed with the folan.

00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:22.570
Wow.

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:23.440
Yeah.

00:16:25.660 --> 00:16:29.980
Hey, Bernard, you ever tried eating like a really big spicy meal right...

00:16:30.030 --> 00:16:32.110
for bedtime. I mean, I find

00:16:32.110 --> 00:16:34.280
when my stomach's upset, my dreams are

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:37.340
more intense. Maybe a couple more of these will help.

00:16:49.140 --> 00:16:50.840
Look, Joel, if you're just doing this because you

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:52.480
think you have to, please don't.

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:54.810
You can find somewhere else to stay.

00:16:56.120 --> 00:16:58.080
Well, to be absolutely honest, Elaine...

00:17:00.960 --> 00:17:02.480
Yeah, I do. I want you to stay.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:04.340
You mean that?

